course: everyday
lesson: 499
title: The only child: all the attention and wanting a sibling
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: With one child, all the parents' attention lands on one person: lovely, and sometimes heavy. In Bangor, North Wales, both Mum and Dad come to every one of Bethan's netball matches and shout «Go on, Beth! Go on, cariad!» («cariad» is Welsh for «love»). When she misses a goal, she feels two pairs of eyes on her. In Birmingham, Nadia's parents check her homework every night and argue about whether her handwriting is neat enough. «I wish I had a brother so you'd have someone else to fuss over!» she snaps, then feels guilty. Parents often don't know that cheering can feel like pressure, so it helps to say kindly, «Can you cheer, but not coach?» or «Can I do it alone and you check on Fridays?»
explain: Some families have one child because they choose to, for money, time, health or just because. In Chicago, Brody asks, «Why'd y'all only have me?» Dad grins: «We got it right first time, why risk it?» Some families wanted more babies and it didn't happen. In Leicester, Teodor, whose parents came from Romania, asks for a baby brother for Christmas. Mum goes quiet, then says, «We wanted more babies, but it didn't happen, puiule» («puiule» is Romanian for «little chick», a pet name). Teodor wishes he hadn't asked. It's okay that he asked; it is private and sad for his parents, and it is not his fault. A hug, and no more pushing.
explain: More presents, fewer people to share them with. In Paisley, near Glasgow, Dougal's pal says, «Ye're lucky, ye get aw the presents.» Dougal says, «Aye, but naebody tae play wi' on Christmas Day» («aw» = all, «naebody» = nobody, «tae» = to, «wi'» = with). In Dunedin, New Zealand, Ngaio's mum and dad play cards with her every night; her mate says, «Chur, my olds never play with me, there's too many of us» («chur» = cool or thanks, «olds» = parents). Parents' time gets shared out between kids, so with one child there's more for each. In Perth, Australia, Lachlan's mate says, «Mate, you're spoilt rotten, you've got every console.» Lachlan is stung, then gives his old Switch to a cousin who hasn't got one. Having a lot is not the same as being spoilt.
explain: Only children can grow up fast in grown-up company. In Cardiff, Celestine, whose parents are both lawyers, says at a sleepover, «Statistically, we'll be tired tomorrow.» The other girls just want a pillow fight. She feels awkward, then joins in and loves it. In Kilkenny, Fionn lives with his mam. «You're my best pal, Fionn,» she says, and every Saturday she wants to go to the shops together, while Fionn wants to play football with the lads. He feels guilty leaving her. It's fine to tell Mam kindly: a good parent wants you to have mates your own age.
explain: In Harrogate, Yorkshire, Pip's three cousins stay for a week of summer: water fights, bunk beds, chips every night, absolute chaos. «It's mint!» says Pip (Northern slang for «brilliant»). When they leave, the house feels empty. By the next morning, Pip's glad of the quiet. Liking noise and liking quiet can both be true. A family with one child is a complete family.
fact: In a family with one child, all the parents' attention goes to that child, which can feel lovely and sometimes like pressure.
fact: Parents may not realise that cheering or checking feels like pressure; telling them kindly usually helps.
fact: Asking for a deal, like checking homework only on Fridays, is a fair way to ask for more trust.
fact: Some parents choose to have one child, for money, time, health or just because.
fact: Some parents wanted more children and it didn't happen; that is private, often sad, and never the child's fault.
fact: Parents' time is shared between their children, so an only child often gets more of it.
fact: Only children may get more presents but have no brother or sister to share the day with; inviting cousins or friends helps.
fact: Having a lot is not the same as being spoilt; being kind and sharing is what matters.
fact: Only children may talk like adults from time with grown-ups, and they still need silly time with kids.
fact: A parent on their own may lean on an only child for company, but a child needs friends of his own age.
fact: It is normal to love company and then want quiet; a family with one child is a complete family.
fact: «Cariad» is Welsh for love, «puiule» is a Romanian pet name meaning little chick, «naebody» is Scots for nobody, «chur» is New Zealand for cool or thanks, «olds» means parents and «mint» is Northern slang for brilliant.
exercise: Why does Bethan feel pressure at netball? | Two pairs of eyes are on her every time | Her coach shouts at her after every match | Her team-mates won't ever pass her the ball | She has never once scored a single goal
exercise: What could Bethan kindly ask her parents? | «Can you cheer, but not coach?» | «Please never come to a match again» | «Can you shout louder at the ref?» | «Can you bring my cousins instead?»
exercise: What deal could Nadia ask for? | «Can I do it alone and you check on Fridays?» | «Can you do all my homework for me from now on?» | «Can we stop having homework in this house?» | «Can you check it twice every single night?»
exercise: Why does Brody's dad say «We got it right first time»? | It's a jokey way of saying they chose one and love him | He means Brody has to be perfect at everything | He means he wanted a dog instead of a second kid | He is cross that Brody asked about it at all
exercise: Teodor's mum says, «We wanted more babies, but it didn't happen.» What should Teodor do? | Give her a hug and not keep pushing | Ask every day until she says yes | Blame himself for being an only child | Tell everyone at school the secret
exercise: «Puiule» is a pet name in Romanian. What does it mean? | Little chick | Little brother | Go to sleep | Happy Christmas
exercise: What does Dougal miss on Christmas Day? | Somebody to play with | More presents under the tree | A much bigger Christmas tree | Snow in the garden
exercise: «Naebody tae play wi'.» What does «naebody» mean? | Nobody | Neighbour | Naughty boy | Everybody
exercise: Why does Ngaio's mate say his parents never play with him? | There are too many kids to share their time | His parents hate playing any kind of cards | He lives in a different country from them | His parents are always at work on Sundays
exercise: What did Lachlan do after being called spoilt? | Gave his old Switch to his cousin | Bought an even newer console | Stopped talking to his mate | Hid all his consoles away
exercise: What does Celestine do in the end at the sleepover? | Joins the pillow fight and loves it | Goes home early to read a big book | Explains statistics till they sleep | Rings her parents to come and get her
exercise: Fionn wants to play football with the lads, and Mam wants him at the shops. What does the lesson say? | Tell Mam kindly; a good parent wants him to have mates | Fionn must always stay with Mam, she has no one else | Fionn should just sneak off without telling her at all | Fionn should give up football so Mam isn't on her own
exercise: How does Pip feel the morning after the cousins leave? | Glad of the quiet | Desperate to move | Sad for a year | Cross with her mum
exercise: Which is true about families with one child? | They are complete families | They are only half families | They are always rich | They are not allowed pets
exercise: «Chur, my olds never play with me.» Who are the «olds»? | His parents | His old toys | His grandparents | Old friends
exercise: Why might one child get more of the parents' time? | Time is shared out between the kids | Parents of one child don't work | The law says so | One child needs more help
test: «only kids get loads of presents so they're always happy lol». What does Dougal show? | More presents can still mean no one to play with | Presents always make only kids totally happy | Only kids get fewer presents than other kids | Christmas is exactly the same for every family
test: «my mum said they couldn't have more babies, is it cos of me?». What is true? | No, it isn't the child's fault | Yes, kids stop babies coming | Only if you asked for a sibling | Yes, if you were a naughty baby
test: «Lachlan's spoilt rotten, he's got every console». What changed the picture? | He shared, and sharing is the opposite of spoilt | He hid the consoles under his bed from his mates | He sold every console he had to buy a scooter | He started bragging even more at school
test: What would probably happen if Nadia told her parents calmly that the checking feels like no trust? | They might agree to a deal, like Fridays | They would never speak to her ever again | They would check twice as often from then on | They would stop helping her with anything
test: Brody's mate says, «Your parents only had you 'cos they couldn't have more». What does the lesson say? | Some parents choose one child, and that is a complete family | Every family with one child wanted more and couldn't have them | Parents with one child always secretly wish they had five | Brody should ask until his parents give him a brother
test: «Celestine talks like a lawyer so she can't have fun». What's true? | Only children may talk grown-up and still need silly time | Kids who talk like adults can never join a pillow fight | Celestine should be told off for using big words at all | Only children never go on sleepovers with other kids
test: In a film, an only child has to look after a lonely mum and never sees friends, and it's shown as normal. What does the lesson say? | A child needs friends his age too | That is exactly how it should be | Mums don't like company anyway | Friends are only for big families
test: ★★★ Ngaio's family plays cards for 30 minutes every night. Her mate's family has 6 kids and the parents have 30 minutes a night to share equally among them. How many minutes a week does each of her mate's siblings get? | 35 | 210 | 5 | 30
test: ★★★ Pip's 3 cousins stay 7 nights and everyone, Pip included, has chips every night. One portion each per night. How many portions in the week? | 28 | 21 | 24 | 7
test: «Pip loved the noise then liked the quiet, so she's fake». What is true? | Liking both noise and quiet is normal | She must choose one and stick to it | She lied about the water fights | She secretly hated her cousins
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the attention afforded to an only child? | It is undivided, which may prove both agreeable and burdensome | It is invariably insufficient owing to the parents' preoccupations | It is, by statute, equal to that afforded to children of large families | It is of no consequence to the child's feelings whatsoever
exam: It is given that parents desired further children but none were born. Identify the correct characterisation of this circumstance. | A private and often sorrowful matter for which the child bears no blame | A consequence of the child having requested a sibling at Christmas | A matter which the child is obliged to raise with the parents daily | A circumstance arising solely from the parents' choice of country
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning possessions and the character of being spoilt. | The possession of many things is not equivalent to being spoilt | The possession of several consoles establishes that a child is spoilt | An only child is by definition spoilt irrespective of conduct | Generosity in sharing is evidence of a spoilt character
exam: Whereas a parent living alone seeks the constant companionship of an only son, state the conduct the lesson recommends. | To inform the parent kindly that he wishes also to see friends of his age | To relinquish all friendships in order to accompany the parent | To absent himself without informing the parent of his whereabouts | To request that the parent cease to visit the shops altogether
